WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.780
Your professional pattern room: tools, fabric and flow.

2
00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:08.510
This is the video for module one of Advanced Tailoring and Pattern Making.

3
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:21.300
At advanced level you are not only sewing clothes. You are making patterns that must be repeated exactly, sometimes for twenty aso-ebi guests or a boutique order of thirty pieces.

4
00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:35.750
Every curve must be smooth and every notch must match its partner. Use the longest curve that fits the line. A hip curve makes one smooth sweep on a princess seam, where a short French curve makes a wobbly line.

5
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:44.390
You will also learn to read fabric before you cut it, and to set up a workroom where each garment moves forward, never backwards.

6
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:56.300
A pattern only you understand is a draft. A pattern a cutter can use without asking questions is a finished pattern. Write the style, then the piece name and size.

7
00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:03.250
Write the cut quantity, for example, cut 2 self, cut 2 lining, or cut 1 on fold.

8
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:12.160
Draw the grain line the full length of the piece, with arrow heads at both ends, so the cutter can measure from the selvedge at two points.

9
00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:24.270
Use one notch on the front armhole and two on the back, so a front sleeve can never go into a back armhole. Punch dart drill holes about 1 cm inside the true point.

10
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:31.640
And state the seam allowance. If anything is missing, the cutter has to guess, and guesses cost fabric.

11
00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:42.130
The lengthwise grain runs parallel to the selvedge, the finished edge. It is the strongest direction and stretches least, so most pieces are cut on it.

12
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:52.630
The true bias is the diagonal at 45 degrees. To find it, fold the crosswise edge along the selvedge. Bias stretches and drapes.

13
00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:58.940
Hang a bias-cut skirt for at least a day before you level the hem, because the bias drops unevenly.

14
00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:13.880
Cotton, linen and many ankara prints shrink when first washed. A fitted gown cut to exact size can become too tight. So test first. Cut a square of 50 cm by 50 cm along the grain.

15
00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:18.630
Mark the lengthwise direction with a thread or a pen arrow.

16
00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:25.880
Wash and dry it the way the client will, for example hand wash and line dry. Then press it.

17
00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:38.240
For example, Bisi in Ibadan tested a cotton print. The lengthwise side now measured 48.5 cm, and the crosswise side 49.5 cm.

18
00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:54.640
50 minus 48.5, divided by 50, times 100, is 3 percent lengthwise. Crosswise it is 1 percent. A 110 cm dress would lose about 3.3 cm after the first wash.

19
00:02:54.990 --> 00:03:00.680
So Bisi washed, dried and pressed the whole fabric before cutting, and wrote it on the order card.

20
00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:11.900
When orders grow, the problem is rarely sewing skill. It is flow. Tag every client's fabric with her name, order number and due date the moment it arrives.

21
00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:22.620
Cut on the largest clear table, then tie all pieces of one garment together with its tag, zip, buttons and lining, so the sewer never searches.

22
00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:31.140
Put a bright white LED lamp at each machine, and keep pressing close by, because you press after almost every seam.

23
00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:41.760
Then finish, inspect and pack. For example, Hauwa in Kaduna timed herself and added an overlocker first, because it removed her slowest step.

24
00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:55.170
Now the safety rules. Do not plug several machines and an iron into one extension board. Overloaded boards can overheat. Use a voltage stabiliser or surge protector for machines.

25
00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:08.220
Run a generator outside, never in a closed room. Its exhaust contains carbon monoxide, which can kill without warning. Store fuel outside, away from a hot generator.

26
00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:20.130
Unplug the iron when you leave the table, and test shiny synthetics on a scrap, because they can melt. Switch off a machine before changing the needle or clearing a jam.

27
00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:30.940
To remember. Use the longest curve that fits the line, and label every finished piece fully. Test shrinkage on a 50 cm square before you cut.

28
00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:37.370
Bundle each garment with its tag. And keep generators outside and boards never overloaded.

29
00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:46.620
Now label one pattern piece fully, make two fabric test cards, map your workroom flow, and write a care card for a recent garment.

30
00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:48.910
Continue with the lessons below.
